          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-e80c60e873c165cb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Christian’s “Je vous aime” is a direct, unqualified avowal presenting his love for Roxane as true. It neither undertakes a specific relational commitment nor exhibits positive evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge; the silence and emotional setting do not independently establish E.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses Roxane while seated beside her; this is the first love avowal in their conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Christian has rejected Cyrano’s offer to prepare or supply his speech and intends to speak for himself. His bare declaration is therefore framed as his own sincere avowal, but Roxane finds it rhetorically inadequate and repeatedly asks him to elaborate. Her dissatisfaction concerns eloquence, not the intelligibility of his assertion of love. Although the scene is courtship and Christian hopes for reciprocation, this particular utterance does not itself establish a commitment or undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CHRISTIAN (s'assied près d'elle, sur le banc. Un silence): Je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense declaration directly presents Christian’s love for Roxane as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before meeting Roxane, Christian says he is tired of borrowing letters and speeches and will now “parler moi-même” (speak for himself).\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is framed as Christian’s own avowal rather than Cyrano’s prompted or revoiced language.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane answers, “Oui, parlez-moi d'amour,” then calls love merely “le thème” and commands, “Brodez, brodez.”\",\"supports\":\"Roxane understands the words as a straightforward assertion of love but demands rhetorical elaboration; this supports T and supplies no independent P or E force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Christian later asks Roxane to say that she loves him, while Roxane concludes, “Vous m'aimez, je sais.”\",\"supports\":\"The exchange treats Christian’s repeated formula as communicating a loving state and a wish for reciprocation, not as making a specific commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as a direct avowal. The scene’s courtship, desire for reciprocation, and comic failure of eloquence are contextual features that do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied analytical translation was used only as an aid; classification is grounded in the French source context.\"}"
        }
